First, I will provide my own solution to the prompt. A classic and efficient way to solve this problem is by using a simple counter. The logic is as follows: iterate through the string, increment the counter for an opening parenthesis `(`, and decrement it for a closing one `)`. If the counter ever drops below zero, or if it's not zero at the end of the string, the sequence is invalid.

Here is the implementation in Java:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Solucao {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        String entrada = sc.nextLine();

        int contador = 0;
        boolean valido = true;

        for (int i = 0; i < entrada.length(); i++) {
            char c = entrada.charAt(i);
            if (c == '(') {
                contador++;
            } else if (c == ')') {
                contador--;
            }

            // If the counter is negative, it means a ')' appeared without a matching '('.
            if (contador < 0) {
                valido = false;
                break; // No need to continue checking.
            }
        }

        // If the loop finishes and the counter is not zero, it means there are unclosed '('.
        if (contador != 0) {
            valido = false;
        }

        if (valido) {
            System.out.println("S");
        } else {
            System.out.println("N");
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}
```

